WEBVTT
TITLE The Tabular Method: Quine–McCluskey, Prime Implicant Charts and Petrick's Method — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A Karnaugh map is a picture of 2 to the n cells. At four variables that is sixteen.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
At five variables it is two maps folded together; at six it needs a third dimension.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
The tabular method is the same search written down, so it has no size limit.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Because it is written, it is also the method a program can run.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Two product terms that differ in one variable are x·y and x·y prime, which sum to x.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
The dash records the variable the merge removed: 0010 with 0110 becomes 0-10.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Only groups whose one-counts differ by one can combine, so the table sorts by that count.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Dashes must line up: -110 with 011- differ in three places and never combine.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
The 2015 paper asks for the prime implicants of sigma m of 0, 2, 4, 5, 6, 10.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Written in binary and sorted by ones: one term with none, two with one, three with two.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Six merges in round one: every term finds a partner, so nothing survives yet.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Round one leaves six dashed terms: 00-0, 0-00, 0-10, -010, 010- and 01-0.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Round two: two pairs combine, both producing 0--0, and two terms stand unticked.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Round three finds nothing: one term in, no pair combines, and the search stops.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Eight merges, three survivors: the answer is 3, which is the published key.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
Three primes covering six minterms: two, three and one of them respectively.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
A prime implicant is an implicant that no larger implicant contains.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
The table finds them by ticking. A ticked term is contained in its child, so it cannot be prime.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Checked against the definition: all eighty-one cubes enumerated, the maximal ones are the three.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Prime is not the same as needed: a prime implicant can still be left out of every cover.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
The chart: one row per prime implicant, one column per minterm of the function.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
A column holding one cross forces its row: minterm 0 is covered only by 0--0.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
All three are essential, they cover all six minterms, and the cover has eight literals.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Two bookkeeping rules shrink a chart before any guessing: row and column dominance.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
GATE CSE 2018 Q49: six minterms and five don't-cares, and the question is essentials.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Don't-cares enter when the terms are listed and leave when the chart is drawn.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Six primes, and one of them covers only don't-cares, so its row is empty.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
Three columns hold one cross — minterms 0, 5 and 9 — so the answer is 3.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Sigma m of 0, 1, 2, 5, 6, 7 over three variables: six primes, each of two minterms.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Every column holds exactly two crosses, so no column forces a row.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Dominance finds nothing either: every row covers two, every column holds two.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Two minimal covers exist and they tie at three primes and six literals.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Petrick's method writes one sum per column: the rows that cover it, or-ed together.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Six sums multiplied out, then absorbed with X plus X Y equals X.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Five products survive, two of them with three terms, and those two are the minima.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Both covers were checked against all eight inputs of the function.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
The procedure: list, group, combine to a fixed point, chart, essentials, then choose.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Eight primes on a four-variable function of eight minterms: the count is not small.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Three traps: don't-cares in the chart, a dash mismatch, and a cover stopped early.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
The table is the map with the picture removed, and the chart is the price of exactness.

